<h2>The number, in context</h2><p>100,000 stars in 45 hours puts DeepSeek Harness in the fastest-growth tier of agent frameworks, a club previously occupied by Claude Code's early ecosystem. For dshbase readers the more useful question is not <em>whether</em> to install, but what the star curve implies for plugin supply.</p><h2>Why plugins are flooding in</h2><ul><li>The bundle manifest format (<code>cordis.patch.yml</code> and friends) makes a valid plugin mostly a packaging exercise — low effort, high reach.</li><li><code>dsh plugin add</code> works straight from a GitHub repo, so authors do not need npm publishing to ship.</li><li>Everything-is-a-plugin means UI skins and workflow tweaks count as plugins, inflating category variety fast.</li></ul><h2>Read the hype with a filter</h2><p>Star velocity also attracts topic squatting: repos tagged <code>dsh-plugin</code> that are not real bundles. This is exactly why our directory verifies installs before marking entries <code>verified</code>, and why the honest advice is: check the plugin page on dshbase before adding anything from a trending list.</p>
<h2>On dshbase</h2>
<p>Before installing random GitHub plugins, check the <a href={directoryUrl}>verified directory</a> and <a href={auditUrl}>audit notes</a>.</p>

